        <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
        <h1>Puneet Mishra</h1>
        <p class="deck">I take things apart to find out how they actually work.</p>

        <p>
          Reverse engineering is the through-line — binaries, bytecode, game
          engines, protocols, and the protection people wrap around them. Ghidra,
          IDA, dnSpy, whatever the target calls for. Injection and hooking, native
          and managed. Unpacking obfuscators, and devirtualising the little VMs
          people hide their logic inside.
        </p>

        <p>
          The rest of it isn't a different interest, it's the same one pointed
          elsewhere: kernels and low-level optimisation, compilers, data
          structures and algorithms, competitive programming, machine learning
          and data science, CTFs and hackathons. Rust, C++, Go, Python, Java —
          the language is whatever the problem is written in.
        </p>

        <p class="note"><b>Also true</b>I will spend a genuinely unreasonable amount of time on a window manager config.</p>

        <p>
          What I write here is whatever was interesting enough to take apart
          that week. The figures are live — the simulations and decoders run in
          your browser off real logic, not screenshots of something that once
          worked.
        </p>

        <p>
          <a href="https://github.com/0xpun337">GitHub</a> ·
          <a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a>
        </p>
      </div>
